## Criticisms (criticisms)
[5b50ded7d2592cb5] (mini.criticism.v1)
All three proposals failed because the proposers misread the rule regarding 'no relaxation'. They assumed that 'no relaxation' meant a character-for-character match was required for the span to occur in the document. However, the rule for `conformance.kernel.grounding` refers to the behavior of the harness, and the source code reveals that the harness applies whitespace normalization to both the span and the document before checking for occurrence.

Specifically, `_grounding_kernel` calls `_span_occurs`, which executes `normal_span, normal_document = _normalise_whitespace(span), _normalise_whitespace(document)` (line 138 of [36a46390d285000e]). Since `_normalise_whitespace` replaces all whitespace sequences (including line breaks) with a single space (line 134 of [36a46390d285000e]), a span differing from the document only by a line break is treated as a verbatim match. This is why the executions for 310463fdc33fc466, f847f44575bd9524, and 386f909160cde68a all resulted in 'unchanged' (GROUNDED before and after), contradicting the proposers' expectation that the initial verdict would be SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT.

These failures matter because they demonstrate a fundamental misunderstanding of how the harness handles whitespace. A proposer should next look for relaxations that are *not* handled by `_normalise_whitespace`, such as case differences in the span-to-document match, as the `_span_occurs` function only applies `.casefold()` if `"case_insensitive"` is in the relaxations list (line 141 of [36a46390d285000e]), and the grounding kernel explicitly uses an empty tuple for relaxations (line 156 of [36a46390d285000e]).

